window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-9ffa6664dba73aa2"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"tasks-and-memory\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">任务与记忆</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>dot（专属智能体）可以同时跟进多项持续性工作。你可以在同一段对话中切换任务、补充细节或调整优先级。说明你需要的结果，以及 dot 应使用哪些信息源。内置安全措施和应用权限会自动生效；你还可以补充更具体的指示。</p> <blockquote> <p>插图：dot 对话中显示两个任务链接、一条后续指示，以及一个已测试、等待审核的拉取请求草稿；对话旁还显示“活动”和“输出”。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"assigned-work\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">已分派的工作</h2> <p>dot 会在两次对话之间继续工作，并根据情况变化跟进。它可以自行判断何时暂停、何时重新开始工作，因此你不必为每次跟进都安排固定日程。你也可以指定某个时间或受支持的事件来触发工作。告诉它哪些结果需要汇报，以及哪些决定需要你参与。</p> <p>dot 可以把工作拆分给并行运行的后台智能体，再接收它们的汇报。它们工作期间，你仍可以继续与 dot 交谈。dot 还可以创建独立、可见的线程并发送后续指示。</p> <p>新的云端线程会显示在桌面、网页和移动应用中，你可以打开线程并继续指导工作。本地 Codex 线程可在已连接的电脑上访问。编程工作需要特定代码仓库和环境配置时，dot 可以使用你创建的 Codex 云端环境。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>任务类型</th> <th>dot 如何处理</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>新建本地 Work 或 Codex 任务</td> <td>在已连接到 dot 的电脑上创建任务。请保持电脑联网，并打开 ChatGPT 应用。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已有的本地 Codex 任务</td> <td>在已连接的电脑上继续任务，并说明任务身份及希望修改的内容。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>新建云端编程任务</td> <td>在已配置好的 Codex 云端环境中创建任务。此时你的电脑可以处于离线状态。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>dot 创建的任务</td> <td>使用该任务原先所在的电脑或云端环境查看结果并发送后续指示。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>如需开展云端编程工作，请先在 Codex 中创建环境，并配置代码仓库和环境设置。更改 dot 选用的电脑，不会把已有任务迁移到那台电脑。</p> <p>在桌面应用中打开 dot 的个人资料，选择 <strong>活动（Activity）</strong>，再打开任务查看进度、文件、结果或待你回应的请求。你也可以直接补充指示。你暂时离开后，dot 仍可继续协调任务。</p> <p>新任务会收到 dot 为这项工作提供的指示和上下文，并拥有独立对话；它不会自动接收你与 dot 的全部对话。连接电脑也不会授予它访问所有既有 ChatGPT 或 Codex 对话的权限。</p> <p>运行结束后，仍应查看输出和报告的错误。任务显示已完成，并不自动证明你要求的结果已经实现或交付。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-e13354c6b025f6aa?anchor=review-work\">查看工作进度</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"recurring-tasks\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">重复任务</h2> <p>需要在固定时间重复执行的工作，必须保存日程安排。请明确说明：</p> <ul> <li>dot 应检查或更新什么。</li> <li>运行时间，包括时区和结束日期（如有）。</li> <li>哪些变化需要通知你。</li> <li>应将结果发送到哪里。</li> </ul> <p>例如：</p> <blockquote> <p>接下来四周，请在每个工作日太平洋时间上午 9 点检查已连接的计划频道。截止日期有变化时更新项目清单。只有截止日期可能无法按时完成，或需要我作决定时，才在 ChatGPT 中联系我。请确认日程安排。</p> </blockquote> <p>让 dot 确认已保存的内容。使用 <strong>已安排（Scheduled）</strong> 查看重复工作；也可以让 dot 列出、修改或取消已安排的任务。</p> <p>dot 还可以读取和编辑你现有的 ChatGPT Work 自动化任务。你可以让它查看已安排的任务，或修改某个重复任务的说明或时间。请明确指出要更改的自动化任务，并让 dot 确认这次更新。</p> <p>停止正在进行的工作和取消日程安排是两项独立操作。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-e13354c6b025f6aa?anchor=stop-work\">控制</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"event-monitoring\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_4\">事件监控</h3> <p>如果已连接的服务支持事件监控，你也可以让 dot 在特定事件发生时采取行动。例如，让它调查已连接 Slack 频道中新出现的错误报告，并准备修复方案供你审核。请说明要监控哪些事件，以及何时通知你；并让它确认实际能够跟踪哪些事件。仅连接 Slack 或其他信息源，不会自动创建监控任务。</p> <p><a id=\"context-and-memory\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">上下文与记忆</h2> <p>dot 会利用当前处理的对话、相关的 ChatGPT 记忆，以及它自行保存的笔记，跟踪你的偏好和长期职责。这些信息各有不同用途。</p> <p><a id=\"conversation-context\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">对话上下文</h3> <p>上下文是 dot 回复或工作时可用的信息，包括消息、指示、相关资料，以及工具或受委派工作的结果。dot 可以利用这些信息，把新请求与正在进行的工作衔接起来。</p> <p>dot 还可以搜索你过去的 ChatGPT 对话，查找相关上下文。例如，你可以让它找到之前的讨论，并在当前任务中参考其中的决定。检索到的对话内容只是当前工作的上下文；它不等同于持久记忆，也不表示每项任务都会自动包含完整的历史记录。</p> <p>每次交互可用的上下文只是上述信息中的一部分。每次模型调用会使用选定的对话上下文，这些内容可能不同于后台任务能够获取的信息。</p> <p><a id=\"persistent-memory\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">持久记忆</h3> <p>dot 会先读取 ChatGPT 记忆中的相关上下文。它还会单独保存有关偏好、决定和进行中工作的笔记，以便在之后的对话中使用。这些笔记与 ChatGPT 的已保存记忆分开，也不是你说过的所有内容的完整记录。</p> <p>协作过程中，dot 可以更新这些笔记，以记录新的决定和优先级变化。更改 ChatGPT 的已保存记忆设置，不一定会修改 dot 已经记录的笔记。</p> <p><a id=\"across-messaging-channels\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_8\">跨消息渠道</h3> <p>ChatGPT、Slack 和 Teams 都可以联系同一个 dot。在它们之间切换不会创建另一个 dot，也不会重置已保存的笔记。你通过一种联系方式分享的信息，可以帮助 dot 在另一种渠道中开展工作或回复。</p> <p>各渠道中显示的对话仍然彼此独立。ChatGPT 中的消息不会自动复制到 Slack 对话。dot 能够使用某项信息，也不代表它获准向其他对象披露该信息。例如，它可以利用你私下分享的截止日期帮助你准备进展更新；但若要在团队频道中分享私人细节，仍须得到许可。</p> <p>这种连续性适用于你与 dot 的对话。连接电脑并不会让 dot 自动访问所有应用中的每段对话。联系方式和可用性请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-4b4aabd54f9e5c9c\">给 dot 发消息</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"proactive-research\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">主动研究</h2> <p>除已分派的工作外，dot 还可以研究获准读取的信息，并将有用发现记录在私密笔记中。这种后台研究可以把新信息与之前的工作联系起来。例如，dot 可能会提醒你，某项发布决定与上周分享的草稿存在冲突。</p> <p>主动研究本身不会发送消息、修改已连接应用，也不会控制浏览器或电脑。后台智能体会把发现汇报给 dot，再由 dot 向你提出建议或问题。任何后续操作仍受该项工作适用的权限和审批要求约束。</p> <p>已分派的重复任务可以包含你授权的操作；这类任务的权限和日程安排与主动研究彼此独立。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"tasks-and-memory\">Tasks and memory</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Your dot can keep track of more than one responsibility at a time. You can switch tasks, add a detail, or change priorities in the same conversation. Describe the result you need and the sources it should use. Built-in safeguards and app permissions apply automatically; you can add more specific instructions.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: A dot conversation shows two task links, a follow-up instruction, and a tested draft pull request ready for review. Activity and Outputs appear alongside the conversation.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"assigned-work\">Assigned work</h2> <p>Your dot keeps working between conversations and follows through as things change. It can decide when to pause and wake up to continue, so you don’t need to put every follow-up on a fixed schedule. You can also specify a time or a supported event that should trigger work. Tell it which results deserve an update and which decisions need your input.</p> <p>Your dot can divide work among background agents that run in parallel and report back to it. You can keep talking to your dot while they work. It can also create separate, visible threads and send follow-up instructions.</p> <p>New cloud threads appear in your desktop, web, and mobile apps, where you can open them and continue steering the work. Local Codex threads are available on the connected computer. For coding work that needs a particular repository and setup, your dot can use a Codex cloud environment you’ve created.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Task</th> <th>How your dot can work with it</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>New local Work or Codex task</td> <td>Create a task on a computer connected to your dot. Keep that computer online with the ChatGPT app open.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Existing local Codex task</td> <td>Continue the task on a connected computer. Identify the task and what you want to change.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>New cloud coding task</td> <td>Create a task in a Codex cloud environment you have already set up. Your computer can be offline.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Task your dot created</td> <td>Check results and send follow-up instructions using the task's original computer or cloud environment.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>For cloud coding work, create the environment in Codex first, including its repository and setup configuration. Changing your dot's selected computer doesn't move an existing task to that computer.</p> <p>In the desktop app, open your dot's profile, select <strong>Activity</strong>, and open a task to inspect its progress, files, results, or requests for input. You can also give instructions directly. Your dot can continue coordinating the task when you step away.</p> <p>A new task receives instructions and context from your dot for that work. It has its own conversation; it doesn't automatically receive every conversation you've had with your dot. Connecting a computer doesn't grant access to every existing ChatGPT or Codex conversation.</p> <p>Review the output and any reported errors even after a run completes. A completed run doesn't by itself confirm that the requested result was achieved or delivered. See <a href=\"#/article/a-e13354c6b025f6aa?anchor=review-work\">Review work</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"recurring-tasks\">Recurring tasks</h2> <p>Work that repeats at fixed times needs a saved schedule. Specify:</p> <ul> <li>What your dot should check or update.</li> <li>When it should run, including the time zone and any end date.</li> <li>Which changes deserve a notification.</li> <li>Where to deliver results.</li> </ul> <p>For example:</p> <blockquote> <p>Check the connected planning channel each weekday at 9 AM Pacific for the next four weeks. Update the project checklist when a deadline changes. Message me in ChatGPT only if a deadline is at risk or you need a decision. Confirm the schedule.</p> </blockquote> <p>Ask your dot to confirm what it saved. Use <strong>Scheduled</strong> to review recurring work, or ask your dot to list, change, or cancel its scheduled tasks.</p> <p>Your dot can also read and edit your existing ChatGPT Work automations. Ask it to review what's scheduled or change a recurring task's instructions or timing. Identify the automation you want to change and ask it to confirm the update.</p> <p>Stopping active work and canceling a schedule are separate actions. See <a href=\"#/article/a-e13354c6b025f6aa?anchor=stop-work\">Controls</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"event-monitoring\">Event monitoring</h3> <p>When a connected service supports event monitoring, you can instead ask your dot to respond to a specific event. For example, ask it to investigate new bug reports in a connected Slack channel and prepare fixes for your review. Tell it which events to watch for and when to notify you. Ask it to confirm which events it can follow. Connecting Slack or another source alone doesn't create a monitoring task.</p> <h2 id=\"context-and-memory\">Context and memory</h2> <p>Your dot uses the conversation it is working in, relevant ChatGPT memory, and its own saved notes to keep track of your preferences and ongoing responsibilities. These serve different purposes.</p> <h3 id=\"conversation-context\">Conversation context</h3> <p>Context is the information available to your dot while it responds or works: messages, instructions, relevant source material, and results from its tools or delegated work. It can use this information to connect a new request to work already underway.</p> <p>Your dot can search your past ChatGPT conversations for relevant context. For example, ask it to find an earlier discussion and use the decisions from it in your current task. Retrieved conversation content is context for the work; it isn't the same as persistent memory or a complete history included in every task.</p> <p>The context available for an interaction is a selection of this information. Calls use selected conversation context, which can differ from the information available to a task working in the background.</p> <h3 id=\"persistent-memory\">Persistent memory</h3> <p>Your dot starts with relevant context from ChatGPT memory. It also keeps its own notes about preferences, decisions, and ongoing work so it can use them in later conversations. These notes are separate from ChatGPT's saved memory and are not a complete transcript of everything you've said.</p> <p>As you work together, your dot can update these notes to reflect new decisions and changing priorities. Changing a ChatGPT saved-memory setting doesn't necessarily change the notes your dot has already made.</p> <h3 id=\"across-messaging-channels\">Across messaging channels</h3> <p>ChatGPT, Slack, and Teams are ways to reach the same dot. Switching between them doesn't create a separate dot or reset its saved notes. Information you share through one contact method can inform its work and replies through another.</p> <p>The visible conversations remain distinct. A message in ChatGPT isn't automatically copied into your Slack conversation. Your dot's ability to use information also doesn't grant permission to disclose it to another audience. For example, it can use a deadline you shared privately to help you prepare an update, but sharing private details in a team channel still requires permission.</p> <p>This continuity applies to conversations with your dot. Connecting a computer doesn't make every conversation in every app available to it. See <a href=\"#/article/a-4b4aabd54f9e5c9c\">Message your dot</a> for contact methods and availability.</p> <h2 id=\"proactive-research\">Proactive research</h2> <p>Alongside assigned work, your dot can research information it has permission to read and keep private notes about useful findings. This background research can connect new information to earlier work. For example, your dot might flag that a release decision conflicts with a draft you shared last week.</p> <p>Proactive research itself doesn't send messages, change connected apps, or control a browser or computer. The background agents report their findings to your dot, which can bring you a suggestion or question. Any follow-up action is subject to the permissions and approvals that apply to that work.</p> <p>Assigned recurring tasks can include actions you've authorized. Their permissions and schedule are separate from proactive research.</p>"};
